\nL34: suf·​frage 【16†ˈsə-frij 】\nL35: \nL36: sometimes\nL37: \nL38: 【17†-fə-rij 】\nL39: \nL40: 【18†Synonyms of suffrage】\nL41: \nL42: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL43: \nL44: 1\nL45: \nL46: : a short intercessory prayer usually in a series\nL47: \nL48: 2\nL49: \nL50: : a vote given in deciding a controverted question or electing a person for an office or trust\nL51: \nL52: 3\nL53: \nL54: : the right of voting : 【19†franchise】\nL55: \nL56: also : the exercise of such right\nL57: \nL58: 【20†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL59: ## Did you know?\nL60: Why would a 17th-century writer warn people that a chapel was only for “private or secret suffrages”? Because suffrage has been used since the 14th century to mean “prayer” (especially a prayer requesting divine help or 【21†intercession】). So how did suffrage come to mean “a vote” or “the right to vote”? In answering that question, we get a lesson about the ways Latin words enter English.\nL61: The Latin word suffrāgium has a number of vote-related meanings, including “a vote cast in an assembly” and “the right to vote.” In Medieval Latin, this same word had expanded to mean “vote, selection, aid, support, intercessory prayer,” and it’s this suffrāgium that gave us the prayer kind of suffrage in the 14th century.\nL62: It wasn’t until the 16th century that English speakers mined the older—the classical—Latin suffrāgium for a word to use with regard to voting, and especially to refer to the right to vote.\nL63: ## Synonyms of suffrage\nL64: \nL65: Relevance\nL66: \nL67:   * 【22†vote】\nL68:   * 【23†ballot】\nL69:   * 【19†franchise】\nL70: \nL71: 【18†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL72: ## Examples of suffrage in a Sentence\nL73: \nL74: women who fought for suffrage even as the world entered the 21st century, some nations still did not permit women's suffrage\nL75: \nL76: Recent Examples on the Web\nL77: \nL78: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【24†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL79: During the state elections in 1967, Jagdish Master, a teacher turned activist, challenged the veto of the landlords over Dalit suffrage by escorting a group of Dalits to a polling booth in Ekwari, a village in Bihar’s Bhojpur district. — Asim Ali, Time, 24 Oct. 2026 August 26th, 1970 Feminists march in New York City on August 26, 1970 on the 50th anniversary of the passing of the Nineteenth Amendment which granted American women full suffrage. — Literary Hub, 16 Oct.\nL80: 2026 Targeted by white supremacists Election Day of 1871 was one of the most consequential in the history of Philadelphia, at the time an epicenter for the simmering tensions surrounding Black suffrage. — Timothy Welbeck, The Conversation, 13 Oct. 2026 She’s shown using her freedom to open a wildlife refuge for animals, host lavish dinner parties for dozens of guests, and fight for suffrage. — Ben Travers, IndieWire, 18 Sep. 2026 【25†See All Example Sentences for suffrage】\nL81: ## Word History\nL82: \nL83: Etymology\nL84: Middle English, \"help, aid, intercessory prayer, indulgence,\" borrowed from Middle French & Medieval Latin; Middle French, borrowed from Medieval Latin suffrāgium \"vote, selection, aid, support, intercessory prayer,\" going back to Latin, \"vote cast in an assembly, right to vote, decision reached by a vote, influence exerted in support of a candidate or policy,\" from suffrāgor, suffrāgārī \"to express public support (for a candidate, measure, etc.), be favorable (toward)\" (from suf-, assimilated form of sub- 【26†sub-】 + -frāg-, probably from the base of frangere, past participle frāctus, \"to break, shatter\") + -ium, deverbal suffix of function or state — more at 【27†break entry 1】\nL85: Note: Senses of suffrage having to do with voting were borrowed directly from classical Latin from the 16th century onward. The older literal meaning of Latin suffrāgārī that presumably underlies the attested senses having to do with political support and voting is obscure. Though the identity of suf- is clear, the element -frāg- has been subject to varying analyses.\nL86: The most commonly accepted view sees -frāg- —despite the unexpected vowel length—as representing the verb frangere, though the import is not obvious. The idea that the reference is to the use of broken pieces of tile or pottery in voting is unlikely in view of what is known of earlier Roman election practices; moreover, the primary meaning of the verb suffrāgārī is more oriented toward support of a candidate than the mechanics of voting.\nL87: In a revival of an older analysis Jyri Vaahtera connects -frāg- with the noun fragor \"noise of breaking, crash, noisy clamor, shouting,\" and assumes that the verb alluded to the noise of an armed assembly banging weapons as a sign of acclamation (see \"The origin of Latin suffrāgium,\" Glotta, vol. 61 [1993], pp. 66-80.)\nL88: First Known Use\nL89: \nL90: 14th century, in the meaning defined at 【28†sense 1】\nL91: \nL92: Time Traveler\nL93: \nL94: The first known use of suffrage was in the 14th century\nL95: \nL96: 【29†See more words from the same century】\nL97: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL98: \nL99: 【30†suffraganship】\nL100: \nL101: suffrage\nL102: \nL103: 【31†suffragette】\nL104: \nL105: 【32†See all Nearby Words】\nL106: \nL107: ## Articles Related to suffrage\nL108: \nL109: 【33†The Words of the Week - 8/21/20 Some of the words that defined the week ending August 21, 2020】\nL110: \nL111: ## Podcast\nL112: \nL113: ## Cite this Entry\nL114: \nL115: Style\nL116: \nL117: [Select]\nL118: \nL119: “Suffrage.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/suffrage. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL120: \nL121: 【34†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL122: ## Kids Definition\nL123: \nL124: suffrage\nL125: \nL126: noun\nL127: \nL128: suf·​frage 【17†ˈsəf-rij 】\nL129: \nL130: : the right of voting\nL131: \nL132: also : the exercise of such right\nL133: ## Legal Definition\nL134: \nL135: suffrage\nL136: \nL137: noun\nL138: \nL139: suf·​frage 【17†ˈsə-frij 】\nL140: \nL141: 1\nL142: \nL143: : a vote in deciding a controverted question or the choice of a person for an office or trust\nL144: \nL145: no State…shall be deprived of its equal suffrage in the Senate—U.S. Constitution art. V\nL146: \nL147: 2\nL148: \nL149: : the right of voting : 【35†franchise】\nL150: \nL151: also : the exercise of such right\nL152: \nL153: Etymology\nL154: \nL155: Latin suffragium vote, political support, from suffragari to support with one's vote\nL156: ## More from Merriam-Webster on suffrage\nL157: \nL158: Britannica.com: 【36†Encyclopedia article about suffrage†www.britannica.com】\nL159: \nL160: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL161: \nL162: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL163: \nL164: Last Updated: 25 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL165: \nL166: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL167: \nL168: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL169: \nL170: #### 【3†satiate】\nL171: \nL172: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL173: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL174: \nL175: 【4†See More】\nL176: \nL177: 【37†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL178: \nL179: 【38†Merriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate】\nL180: \nL181: 【39†'Affect' vs. 'Effect'】\nL182: \nL183: 【40†'A' or 'an'? What about before 'h'? An Indefinite Article Guide】\nL184: \nL185: 【41†Why is '-ed' sometimes pronounced at the end of a word?】\nL186: ## Popular in Wordplay\nL187: \nL188: 【8†See More】\nL189: \nL190: 【42†The Longest Long Words List】\nL191: \nL192: 【43†9 Words That Sound Like Insults But Aren't】\nL193: \nL194: 【44†Pwn, Skibidi, Girlboss and Other Words Added to the 2026 Scrabble Dictionary】\nL195: \nL196: 【45†Ten More Words from Taylor Swift Songs】\nL197: \nL198: 【46†12 Lovely and Unusual Words for the Natural World】\nL199: \nL200: ## Popular\nL201: \nL202: 【8†See More】\nL203: \nL204: 【37†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL205: \nL206: 【42†The Longest Long Words List】\nL207: \nL208: 【43†9 Words That Sound Like Insults But Aren't】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nREPRESENTATION Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/representation)\nciteturn51view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/representation\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 315\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Games】\nL5:   * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL6:   * 【4†Grammar】\nL7:   * 【5†Word Finder】\nL8:   * 【6†Slang】\nL9:   * 【7†Newsletters】\nL10:   * 【8†Wordplay】\nL11:   * 【9†Rhymes】\nL12:   * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL13:   * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL14:   * More\nL15:     * 【2†Games】\nL16:     * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL17:     * 【4†Grammar】\nL18:     * 【8†Wordplay】\nL19:     * 【6†Slang】\nL20:     * 【9†Rhymes】\nL21:     * 【5†Word Finder】\nL22:     * 【7†Newsletters】\nL23:     * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL24:     * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL25:     * Shop\nL26:     * 【12†Books†shop.merriam-webster.com】\nL27:     * 【13†Merch†merriamwebster.threadless.com】\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Dictionary】\nL30: \nL31:   * # representation\nL32: ## 【15†noun】\nL33: \nL34: rep·​re·​sen·​ta·​tion 【16†ˌre-pri-ˌzen-ˈtā-shən 】\nL35: \nL36: -zən-\nL37: \nL38: plural representations\nL39: \nL40: 【17†Synonyms of representation】\nL41: \nL42: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL43: \nL44: 1\nL45: \nL46: : someone or something that 【18†represents】: such as\nL47: \nL48: a\nL49: \nL50: : an artistic likeness or image\nL51: \nL52: stylized representations of flowers\nL53: The women then embroider their portraits in any way they choose, taking agency over their ultimate representation …—Charleston Regional Business Journal\nL54: \nL55: b(1)\nL56: \nL57: : a statement or account made to influence opinion or action\nL58: \nL59: Her representation of the situation was very confusing.\nL60: \nL61: (2)\nL62: \nL63: : an incidental or collateral statement of fact on the faith of which a contract is entered into\nL64: \nL65: a contract that contained certain representations by the seller\nL66: \nL67: c\nL68: \nL69: : a dramatic production or performance\nL70: a theatrical representation\nL71: \nL72: d(1)\nL73: \nL74: : a usually formal statement made against something or to effect a change\nL75: \nL76: (2)\nL77: \nL78: : a usually formal protest\nL79: \nL80: 2\nL81: \nL82: : the act or action of representing : the state of being represented: such as\nL83: \nL84: a\nL85: \nL86: : 【19†representationalism sense 2】\nL87: \nL88: b(1)\nL89: \nL90: : the action or fact of one person standing for another so as to have the rights and obligations of the person represented\nL91: \nL92: (2)\nL93: \nL94: : the substitution of an individual or class in place of a person (such as a child for a deceased parent)\nL95: \nL96: c(1)\nL97: : the action of representing or the fact of being represented in a grouping, aggregation, or body (especially a legislative body)\nL98: \nL99: The state is divided into legislative districts, with each district electing one senator. This system fosters representation from various regions of the state …—Manahil Ahmad\nL100: \nL101: (2)\nL102: \nL103: : the participation of people from groups who have historically been excluded or discriminated against (as due to race, gender, sexuality, or 【20†ability】)\nL104: Although public libraries are no longer segregated by law, there is still a major gap in African Americans' representation in the library profession.—Terry B. Hill\nL105: \nL106: also : the inclusion of characters, perspectives, etc. from such groups\nL107: \nL108: So, one of my passions, especially in the entertainment industry, is to create more authentic disability representation, because we don't usually see it in mainstream media. —Maddy Ulman, quoted in The Dallas Morning News\nL109: \nL110: d\nL111: : the action of presenting or describing a person or thing especially in a particular way\nL112: \nL113: We discussed the representation of women in Jane Austen's novels.\nL114: \nL115: 3\nL116: \nL117: : the body of persons representing a constituency\nL118: \nL119: representational\nL120: \nL121: 【21†ˌre-pri-ˌzen-ˈtā-sh(ə-)nəl 】\nL122: \nL123: -zən-\nL124: \nL125: adjective\nL126: \nL127: representationally adverb\nL128: ## Examples of representation in a Sentence\nL129: \nL130: Each state has equal representation in the Senate. The letters of the alphabet are representations of sounds.\nL131: \nL132: Recent Examples on the Web\nL133: \nL134: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【22†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL135: Independents are the least impressed by current representation in Washington; only 36% say their representative should be reelected, while 9% say most should be. — Morgan Chalfant, semafor.com, 28 Oct. 2026 The change affected more than 16,000 detainees last month, the lawsuit says, delaying legal filings and limiting representation as the government prioritizes deportations. — Ryan J. Foley, Los Angeles Times, 28 Oct.\nL136: 2026 The others asserted vested development rights and reliance on county representations, leaving only the comprehensive plan challenge, which asserted that commissioners wrongly rejected housing that complied with the county’s growth policies. — Sarah Kinbar, The Orlando Sentinel, 28 Oct. 2026 Perhaps this kind of authentic representation of disabled life could be a fixture that younger generations will one day take for granted. — Rebekah Taussig, Time, 28 Oct.\nL137: 2026 【23†See All Example Sentences for representation】\nL138: ## Word History\nL139: \nL140: First Known Use\nL141: \nL142: 15th century, in the meaning defined at 【24†sense 1】\nL143: \nL144: Time Traveler\nL145: \nL146: The first known use of representation was in the 15th century\nL147: \nL148: 【25†See more words from the same century】\nL149: \nL150: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL151: \nL152: 【26†representant】\nL153: \nL154: representation\nL155: \nL156: 【27†representational】\nL157: \nL158: 【28†See all Nearby Words】\nL159: ## Cite this Entry\nL160: \nL161: Style\nL162: \nL163: [Select]\nL164: \nL165: “Representation.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/representation. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL166: \nL167: 【29†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL168: ## Kids Definition\nL169: \nL170: representation\nL171: \nL172: noun\nL173: \nL174: rep·​re·​sen·​ta·​tion 【16†ˌrep-ri-ˌzen-ˈtā-shən 】\nL175: \nL176: 1\nL177: \nL178: : one (as a picture or symbol) that represents something else\nL179: \nL180: 2\nL181: \nL182: : the act or action of representing : the state of being represented (as in a legislative body)\nL183: \nL184: representational\nL185: \nL186: 【21†ˌrep-ri-ˌzen-ˈtā-shnəl 】\nL187: \nL188: -shən-ᵊl\nL189: \nL190: adjective\nL191: ## Legal Definition\nL192: \nL193: representation\nL194: \nL195: noun\nL196: \nL197: rep·​re·​sen·​ta·​tion\nL198: \nL199: 1\nL200: \nL201: : one that represents: as\nL202: \nL203: a\nL204: \nL205: : a statement or account made to influence opinion or action compare 【30†warranty sense 3】\nL206: \nL207: b\nL208: \nL209: : an incidental or collateral statement of fact on the faith of which a contract is entered into\nL210: \nL211: when a contract for sale contains certain representation s by the purchaser\nL212: \nL213: specifically : a statement of fact made by an applicant to an insurer for the purpose of obtaining insurance\nL214: \nL215: 2\nL216: : the act or action of representing: as\nL217: \nL218: a\nL219: \nL220: : the action or fact of one person standing for another so as to have the rights and obligations of the person represented\nL221: \nL222: b\nL223: \nL224: : the substitution of an individual or class in place of a person (as when a child or children take the share of an estate that would have fallen to a deceased parent) see also 【31†per stirpes】\nL225: \nL226: c\nL227: \nL228: : the action of representing or the fact of being represented especially in a legislative body\nL229: \nL230: d\nL231: : the act or action by a lawyer of providing legal advice to a client and appearing (as in court) to speak and act on the client's behalf\nL232: \nL233: 3\nL234: \nL235: : the body of persons representing a constituency\nL236: ## More from Merriam-Webster on representation\nL237: \nL238: Thesaurus: 【17†All synonyms and antonyms for representation】\nL239: \nL240: Britannica.com: 【32†Encyclopedia article about representation†www.britannica.com】\nL241: \nL242: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL243: \nL244: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL245: \nL246: Last Updated: 29 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL247: \nL248: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL249: \nL250: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL251: \nL252: #### 【3†satiate】\nL253: \nL254: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL255: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL256: \nL257: 【4†See More】\nL258: \nL259: 【33†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL260: \nL261: 【34†Merriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate】\nL262: \nL263: 【35†'Affect' vs. 'Effect'】\nL264: \nL265: 【36†'A' or 'an'? What about before 'h'? An Indefinite Article Guide】\nL266: \nL267: 【37†Why is '-ed' sometimes pronounced at the end of a word?】\nL268: ## Popular in Wordplay\nL269: \nL270: 【8†See More】\nL271: \nL272: 【3